note Translation of matchChunk from Go's path package (go/src/path/match.go).
note Go strings are byte sequences, so chunk and s are Bytes here, not Text.
note UNCLEAR: the doc never says what `item i of` gives for Bytes, or how one byte is typed. Read here as a Byte, an Int with value 0 through 255.

type Byte is an Int with value 0 through 255
type Rune is an Int with value 0 through 1114111

note WORKAROUND: getEsc and utf8.DecodeRuneInString each return two values. Scribe has no tuples or multiple results, so each pair is a record.
type Escape
    field value as a Rune
    field next as a Nat

type DecodedRune
    field value as a Rune
    field width as a Nat

note getEsc: reads one possibly escaped character of a class starting at position.
to get_esc chunk from position
    given chunk is Bytes
    given position is a Nat
    produces an Escape
    fails with BadPattern

note utf8.DecodeRuneInString applied to the part of s starting at position.
to decode_rune_in_string s from position
    given s is Bytes
    given position is a Nat
    produces a DecodedRune

to match_chunk chunk against s using memory
    given chunk is Bytes
    given s is Bytes
    given memory is an Allocator
    produces a maybe Bytes
    fails with BadPattern, OutOfMemory
    uses memory
    note Go returns (rest, ok, err). Here ok false is `nothing`, ok true is `some rest`, and err is a failure.
    note WORKAROUND: Go returns rest as a slice of s, which allocates nothing. Scribe has no slice or view, so rest is copied, and a function that is allocation-free in Go gains `uses memory` and OutOfMemory.

    note failed records whether the match has failed.
    note After the match fails, the loop continues on processing chunk,
    note checking that the pattern is well-formed but no longer reading s.
    note UNCLEAR: the doc never shows boolean literals. `false` and `true` are assumed.
    note WORKAROUND: Go re-slices chunk and s (chunk = chunk[1:]). Parameters cannot be changed and there is no slicing, so cursors ci and si mark where the remaining chunk and s begin.
    var failed starts at false
    var ci starts at 0
    var si starts at 0
    repeat while ci is less than count of chunk
        decreases count of chunk - ci
        set failed to failed or si is count of s
        let c be item ci of chunk
        note WORKAROUND: Go switches on a byte value. `choose` is shown only for choice variants and maybe, so the switch is an if chain, and the backslash case's fallthrough into default is folded into the last branch.
        note GAP: byte literal - the doc has no character or byte literal, so `byte "["` is invented.
        if c is byte "["
            note character class
            var r starts at 0
            if not failed
                let decoded be decode_rune_in_string s from si
                set r to value of decoded
                set si to si + width of decoded
            set ci to ci + 1
            note possibly negated
            var negated starts at false
            if ci is less than count of chunk and item ci of chunk is byte "^"
                set negated to true
                set ci to ci + 1
            note parse all ranges
            var match starts at false
            var nrange starts at 0
            note WORKAROUND: Go's `for { ... break }` becomes `repeat while true` with `stop`. The decreases line holds only if get_esc always moves past position, which Go never states.
            repeat while true
                decreases count of chunk - ci
                if ci is less than count of chunk and item ci of chunk is byte "]" and nrange is more than 0
                    set ci to ci + 1
                    stop
                let low be try get_esc chunk from ci
                let lo be value of low
                var hi starts at lo
                set ci to next of low
                if item ci of chunk is byte "-"
                    let high be try get_esc chunk from ci + 1
                    set hi to value of high
                    set ci to next of high
                set match to match or lo <= r <= hi
                set nrange to nrange + 1
            set failed to failed or match is negated
        otherwise if c is byte "?"
            if not failed
                set failed to item si of s is byte "/"
                let decoded be decode_rune_in_string s from si
                set si to si + width of decoded
            set ci to ci + 1
        otherwise
            note UNCLEAR: the doc gives no escape rules for text literals, so how to write a backslash is a guess.
            if c is byte "\\"
                set ci to ci + 1
                if ci is count of chunk
                    fail with BadPattern
            if not failed
                set failed to item ci of chunk is not item si of s
                set si to si + 1
            set ci to ci + 1
    if failed
        give back nothing
    note UNCLEAR: whether a comprehension over a range is allowed, which allocator it uses, and whether its allocation failure needs `try`.
    let rest be item i of s for each i from si until count of s
    give back some rest
